{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Second-pair-of-eyes review of the recovered draft (AC5). Every line number, file, commit, duration and count was re-checked against the tree at `origin/main` `e510c64dbcb6` or against run `33824556786`. Five assertions did not hold and were corrected; the rest did.\n\n**Held on re-check (no change):** `lock.py:72-75` is the import guard; `:207` and `:254` are the two unguarded `fcntl.flock` uses; `_acquire_mkdir` spans `:325-362` with `fcntl_held=True` at `:362`; `:491` is the `_acquire_fcntl` call on the write path; commit `7024c35790bc` is \"ci: extend periodic sweep with breadth coverage\", 2026-08-26; `_snapshot/git_fetch.py:79` and `_snapshot/janitor.py:430` do guard with `if fcntl is not None`; ADR 0114 does cite incident `90c7-c465-4037-42d5`; story `8d36` is the macOS reduced-subset precedent.\n\n**Corrected:**\n\n**1. Status was `Proposed`.** Every other landed ADR in `docs/adr/` is `Accepted` — `grep -l \"^\\*\\*Status:\\*\\* Proposed\" docs/adr/0*.md` returns nothing on `origin/main`. `Proposed` also contradicted the ADR's own body (\"The operator has adopted…\", \"they are not aspirations\") and the existence of eleven children already filed against the decision. Now `Accepted`, citing the epic and this task, wrapped in the two-line form ADR 0115 uses.\n\n**2. `.github/workflows/test.yml:340-344` is wrong — the block is at `:329-333`.** Cited twice in the draft (Context and Decision §4); both corrected. The stale range was inherited from the bug and the S10 ticket, which were written earlier.\n\n**3. \"384 of the 510 failures\" overstates what the evidence supports.** The epic states plainly that these are counts of `E` assertion/exception lines, \"so they over- and under-count individual tests slightly; they are for ORDERING, not for a burn-down target\". The draft promoted them to a hard subset of the 510. Rewritten to say they are exception-line counts, that a test can emit several and a collection error none, and that `S1` is sized by \"the class is gone\" rather than \"384 fewer failures\" — which matters, because a burn-down reading would make `S1` look partially done at some arbitrary count.\n\n**4. `reducer/marker.py:33` was cited as a module that already guards `fcntl` correctly. It does the opposite.** It guards the import at `:9-11` and then calls `fcntl.flock` unguarded at `:33`, `:38`, `:61` and `:67` — the identical defect. Worse, both call sites sit inside `try/except OSError`, and `AttributeError` is not an `OSError`, so it escapes a function documented to never raise; the `finally` unlock then raises a second `AttributeError` that replaces the first. Citing it as a counter-example would have told the `S1` implementer the rest of the tree was clean. The ADR now names the two genuine guards, names `marker.py` as a second defect site, and points at bug `discrete-unrelative-vervet` (`a50a-cac1-af18-42d9`), filed for it.\n\n**5. \"Windows CI is the slowest and most expensive lane — 662.75s\" is refuted by the run the ADR itself cites.** Job wall times in `33824556786`: `sweep (windows)` 01:13:04Z→01:24:51Z = **11m47s**; `sweep (macos, full suite)` 01:14:00Z→02:06:38Z = **52m38s**. macOS full is ~4.5x the Windows lane. Separately, 662.75s came from a run where 510 tests failed, many during store setup before reaching the work under test, so a *green* Windows run will be longer, not equal. Both errors push the same way — they would have had `S11` budget from a number that is simultaneously too small and attached to the wrong lane. Rewritten to state the measured times, to mark 662.75s a floor, and to require `S11` to re-measure on a green run. Recorded on `S11` (`hillocked-knavish-gander`) as well, since its `file_impact` repeats the same inference.\n\n**6. Minor:** \"eleven children\" — the epic has twelve children; eleven are the numbered `S1`–`S11` and the twelfth is the originating bug. Reworded so a reader running `list --parent` is not confused by the count.\n\n**Also found while doing this, filed rather than folded in:** `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` and `tests/unit/test_fcntl_import_guard.py` both still state that Windows is not a declared support target, and the gate deliberately checks only that the *import* is guarded, explicitly disclaiming any check on the *use* — which is why `make lint` was green on both `lock.py` and `marker.py`. Task `greisen-enjoyable-zethuswasp` (`bf74-a636-e6d9-40f9`). Not folded into this change because it is outside this ticket's reviewed file impact.\n\n**Number re-verification:** 116 markers against 116 ADR files with no `0117` marker on `origin/main`, `scripts/check_adr_numbers.py` exits 0 with the new marker in place, and `docs/adr/README.md` is a fresh `scripts/gen_adr_index.py` run rather than a hand edit."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788575291116937252,"uuid":"fa97bf2f-c755-4c03-ad73-fd32f30ed06f"}